FAIL: any file named is skipped, a bad row is missing or shows a wrong file name or line, output is out of argument order, or the single-file format is not `<file>:<line>: <error>`.

Check 2 — "A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)"
Run `node $CLI - < $T/bad.jsonl`, `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl - < $T/bad.jsonl`, and `node $CLI - - < $T/good1.jsonl`.
PASS: the first two runs report the bad row as `-:2: <error>` (stdin read, named `-`); the `- -` run exits with status 2.
FAIL: stdin is not read for `-`, the bad row is reported under any name other than `-`, or `- -` exits with a status other than 2.

Check 3 — "Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)"
Run: `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl $T/good2.jsonl` (expect 0); `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl $T/bad.jsonl` (expect 1); `node $CLI` (expect 2); `node $CLI $T/good1.jsonl $T/missing.jsonl $T/bad.jsonl` (expect 2).
PASS: the exit statuses are 0, 1, 2 and 2 respectively; in the last run stderr holds an error message about the unreadable file, and stdout still reports `$T/bad.jsonl:2: ...` (the file after the missing one was still checked and reported).
FAIL: any exit status differs, the unreadable-file error is not on stderr, or the bad row of `$T/bad.jsonl` is not reported in the run with a missing file.

Check 4 — "lint(text) in src/index.js is unchanged"
